Host orchestration
------------------

With the test VM already running a matching kernel and mount_btrfs, run:

    ./regress/sys/btrfs/run-all.sh

The runner REFORMATS /home/mike/obj/btrfs.img for each case. It assumes root SSH
access to root@10.77.0.2, /dev/sd1c as the disposable VM disk, /mnt/btrfs as
the mountpoint, /mnt/src as this source tree, and QEMU sockets /tmp/monitor.sock
and /tmp/serial.sock. Override these with --image, --vm, --device, --mountpoint,
--vm-source, --monitor and --serial. It never mounts the image on the host.
Host requirements are Python 3, OpenSSH, timeout and current btrfs-progs with
rootdir, compression and inode-flag support. Host root privileges are unnecessary.
The VM needs root, Python 3 and mount_btrfs; breakpoint recovery cases use amd64
DDB commands and need symbols in the running kernel. The runner does not build
or install a kernel.

The default matrix runs every recipe on four layouts:

    4k           4 KiB nodes, SINGLE data, extent free space
    16k          16 KiB nodes, SINGLE data, separate block-group tree, holes
    4k-dup       4 KiB nodes, DUP data, no free-space tree
    16k-bitmap   16 KiB nodes, DUP data, bitmap free space, holes

All use CRC32C, 4 KiB sectors and DUP metadata. A recipe can override a format
requirement, such as DUP for mirror faults or SINGLE for the small imported
bitmap fixture. Exact mkfs options appear in its command log. Capacity recipes
use small recorded device sizes while retaining the backing file's sparse size.
statfs first fills physical growth capacity and releases a small data range,
so its exact-capacity assertions remain meaningful with chunk growth enabled.

List or select cases without editing the runner:

    ./regress/sys/btrfs/run-all.sh --list
    ./regress/sys/btrfs/run-all.sh --layouts 4k --select namespace
    ./regress/sys/btrfs/run-all.sh --select 'read-*' --select 'recover-*'
    ./regress/sys/btrfs/run-all.sh --exclude '*capacity' --exclude 'recover-*'

--select and --exclude accept shell globs matching either a case name or a
layout/case pair. Quote globs to prevent host shell expansion. The default
includes pressure, deliberate corruption, forced unmount and VM reset tests.
Reset tests launch the guest operation while holding the serial connection,
confirm the requested breakpoint or ready marker, reset with CPUs paused,
check the image independently, then boot and verify recovery.
The debugger handler waits for secondary CPUs to stop and continues delayed
debugger IPIs without counting them as filesystem breakpoint hits.

Results default to a timestamped btrfs-regress directory beside the backing
image; --results chooses another directory. config.json records the kernel,
suite digest and selection. status.json records each case's outcome, duration
and command-log path. Every attempt retains its seed, restore output, corruption
journal and other host artifacts. Commands have bounded timeouts (900 seconds
by default, configurable with --timeout); --boot-timeout bounds reboot polling.
The runner stops at the first failure and preserves the image and VM state.
Each attempt's serial.log continuously records UART output, including panics
while SSH is blocked. commands.log also records monitor diagnostics on failure.

To resume an interrupted run, use the same options and --results directory,
adding --resume. Passed cases are skipped and incomplete cases start with new
fixtures and logs. Resume requires the same kernel, suite contents and selection;
after changing either, use a new results directory and select the desired cases.
--start-at layout/case starts a new run at a chosen point in the selected plan.
An image/VM lock prevents simultaneous runners. All views of the test disk must
be unmounted before starting. Resume unmounts known runner views before restarting
an incomplete case; an unresponsive VM must be recovered before resume.

run_cases.py contains the explicit recipes. When adding a regression or phase,
add its fixture, mutation, independent checks and appropriate remount verification
there. The procedures below remain useful for running an individual phase while
debugging. run_fixtures.py supplies the unprivileged device import fixture by
replacing empty placeholder inodes, directory types and rdev fields in every
metadata copy before the independent checks.

Individual procedures
---------------------

These tests require a disposable btrfs image mounted writable, root, and
Python 3. They leave their files in place for verification after remount.
Use a new test directory for each run.  For example, inside the test VM:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/namespace.py create /mnt/btrfs/ns
    umount /mnt/btrfs

On the host, while the image is unmounted:

    btrfs check --readonly --check-data-csum /path/to/image
    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/mirrors.py /path/to/image

mirrors.py supplements the checker by comparing every allocated metadata copy
and checking that no allocation overlaps a physical superblock stripe.
It supports the writer's SINGLE/DUP format.  The normal checker can recover
from a corrupt DUP copy and still return success, so use both checks.

Then in the VM:

    mount_btrfs /dev/sd1c /mnt/btrfs
    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/namespace.py verify /mnt/btrfs/ns
    umount /mnt/btrfs

flags.py uses create/verify phases and readonly after a read-only remount.
It covers nodump/immutable/append round trips, owner and nonowner permissions,
clearing flags, writes through existing descriptors, append-open policy and
append writes, hard-link
aliases, directory policy, FIFO metadata, rejected flag masks, and kqueue
attribute notifications. Run the independent checks between mounts.

Run all driver-involving SSH commands under timeout.  Repeat with both 4 KiB
and 16 KiB metadata nodes to cover tree growth, and check the resulting image
with btrfs-progs.  The test covers concurrent creation in separate and shared
directories, hash collisions, long names, negative cache invalidation, inode
uniqueness, file data, sparse writes, fsync, permissions, owner/group/mode,
directory size/link accounting, nested parent lookup, and readdir.

checksums.py uses create/verify for sequential and concurrent writes, repeated
pending replacements, checksum-range splitting, and pending tail cancellation.
Run disk on the unmounted image to check checksum packing, alongside both
independent checkers, then verify after writable and read-only remounts.
Cover both metadata node sizes and SINGLE/DUP data.

cluster.py uses create/verify to measure commit output operations and exercise
physical-sector cache eviction while repeatedly freeing and reusing data.
It invalidates vnode buffers before checking data in the same mount. Use create
on 4 KiB nodes with extent free-space records; its total-I/O bound includes
metadata and superblock writes. Use measure on larger nodes or bitmap fixtures,
where metadata can exceed that bound even with clustered data submission.
Run SINGLE/DUP data, then check independently and verify
after both remount modes. checksums.py also covers clustered submission from
concurrent vnodes and canceled sectors; chunks.py covers chunk boundaries.

read_cluster.py uses create in a new VM directory, then measure after a cold
remount to check physical read clustering. OpenBSD input-operation accounting
also includes one logical file-buffer read per sector. Run small, check
independently, then verify_small and again after remount to exercise reuse
between large read windows and sector allocations; use verify_again after
both remount modes. Repeat with 4/16 KiB nodes, SINGLE/DUP data and bitmap
free space. Run reclaim_chunks.py too, to exercise cached windows during
physical data/metadata reassignment.
For imported allocations larger than MAXBSIZE, use `seed directory` on the
host and import with --rootdir. Run measure and split at the mountpoint,
check independently, then verify_split after both remount modes.
Use seed_compressed with --compress zstd, check disk_compressed while
unmounted, and run compressed after cold remounts to check multi-sector
compressed allocations.
For mirror recovery, use a fresh create fixture with DUP data. Unmount,
run `damage image journal` on the host, then mount read-only and run faults
in the VM. It checks recovery from different mirrors of the same window,
EIO when both copies of a sector are damaged, and buffer-size recovery after
the sector retry. Unmount and run `repair image journal` before independent
checks or further clean remounts; measure must pass again afterward.

coalesce.py uses create on a fresh image, then disk while unmounted to verify
that sequential sectors became larger regular extents. Check independently,
verify_original after remount, then mutate to exercise partial/interior COW,
overwrites across extent boundaries, repeated pending replacement, mapped
truncate retention, sparse regrowth, hard links, cancellation, and final drops.
Use verify after writable and read-only remounts. Repeat with both node sizes,
SINGLE/DUP data, explicit holes and bitmap free space; run the checksum,
cluster, truncate, orphan and space-pressure suites against coalescing too.
For publication tests use a fresh create fixture, break at
btrfs_write_super_mirrors, run crash_write, and reset before the superblock
write. Check independently and use verify_original after remount. Repeat
crash_write with a breakpoint at btrfs_space_publish_chunk to reset after
durable publication, then check independently and use verify_crash.

Writable images must meet the feature restrictions in
sys/btrfs/WRITE_SUPPORT.md.  The host must not mount an untrusted image.

Free-space-tree and block-group-tree writes use the existing namespace, idle,
statfs, and enospc tests. Run on fresh 4 KiB and 16 KiB SINGLE/DUP images with
both features enabled, and with `-O ^block-group-tree,^no-holes` to cover
free-space-tree updates alongside extent-tree block groups and explicit holes.
Run statfs exercise/capacity before metadata exhaustion, then unmount and run
both independent checkers (including btrfs check's free-space-tree pass).
Verify namespace/data after writable and read-only remounts. statfs disk
selects the block-group item tree using the superblock feature flag.

chunks.py uses create/verify to write past the initial data capacity from four
processes, check increasing statfs capacity, and verify every sector after
writable/read-only remounts. Test SINGLE/DUP data, both metadata node sizes,
legacy/separate block groups, and imported bitmap free space. Its capacity
phase on a fresh small image fills all allocatable device gaps, checks ENOSPC
without changing the file or making the mount read-only, and reclaims/reuses
the data. Existing statfs capacity tests require a device with no room for
additional data chunks now. metadata/metadata_verify fill metadata through
chunk growth and final device exhaustion, then check the namespace after
remount. combined/combined_verify exercise concurrent data and metadata growth.
On a fresh unmounted image, chunks_fixture.py image shrinks the initial system
group while maintaining all chunk/device/block-group/free-space records and
superblock copies. Check that fixture independently, then run create or
combined to force protected system growth and bootstrap-array publication.
Cover both node sizes and SINGLE/DUP with legacy and separate block groups.
Run hold, wait for /tmp/btrfs-chunks-ready, and
reset the VM to test durability of newly allocated mappings; verify after
mounting again. Run both independent checkers while unmounted.

reclaim_chunks.py exercises reassignment on a small disposable image (128 MiB
for SINGLE data, 256 MiB for DUP).
Run prepare in the VM, unmount, then snapshot image json on the host. Mount and
run free_data followed by metadata, unmount, check independently, and run
reassigned image json DATA METADATA to verify returned logical groups and reused
physical stripes. Use verify_metadata after writable and read-only remounts.
For the reverse direction use metadata_bounded on a fresh prepared fixture,
which leaves namespace-removal headroom; snapshot its unmounted layout, then
run free_metadata and data_again in the VM. Check independently and run
reassigned image json METADATA DATA, then verify_data after both remount modes.
The metadata phases sample statfs concurrently with group removal. Repeat with
4/16 KiB nodes, SINGLE/DUP data, legacy/separate block groups, and bitmap imports.
For bitmap retirement, unmount after free_data and use free_space.py bitmap-groups
image to convert a single free-space leaf containing the grown empty groups;
check independently before continuing. This exercises additional deletion
reservations for groups containing several bitmap records.
To check durable retirement before in-memory publication, break first at
btrfs_free_chunk_items, switch to btrfs_space_publish_chunk, and reset when the
removal commit stops there. Independently check before remounting.

free_space.py's host `seed directory` phase prepares imported data. Format
with `mkfs.btrfs --rootdir directory`, then run `bitmap image` while unmounted
to encode the fresh free-space leaf as bitmaps. Short first bitmaps ensure
data allocations cross bitmap boundaries. Run both independent checks before
mounting, then `exercise mountpoint` in the VM, followed by idle, namespace,
statfs exercise/capacity, and metadata enospc. Check independently after unmount
and verify data after both remount modes, with both metadata node sizes.
On separate fresh bitmap fixtures, `bad-count image` and `bad-bit image`
introduce inconsistent metadata. Run `reject device mountpoint` in the VM;
repeated writable mounts must fail while read-only imported data remains
accessible. The bad-bit case preserves the run count, exercising validation
against extent ownership in addition to structural count checks.

unlink.py uses create/verify/readonly phases for nonfinal-link removal.
It covers ordinary and extended packed references, hash collisions across
commits, inode identity, open-descriptor writes, cache invalidation, concurrent
removal, symlink/FIFO/device aliases, directory accounting, flags, sticky and
ordinary permissions, and notifications. On a small fixture, `capacity new-directory` fills metadata and
checks protected namespace removal after exhausting creation and write
reservations, packed reference/hash neighbors, open descriptors and mappings,
last-close cleanup, and ordinary reuse. Use capacity_verify after remount.
Test both metadata sizes,
with and without free-space/block-group trees, and check the unmounted images
before writable and read-only verification.

orphan_cleanup.py uses create/verify for final-link removal, fragmented range
cleanup, writes and attribute changes through unlinked descriptors, mapped
reads, inode-number reuse prevention, and symlink/FIFO/device cleanup.
Its capacity phase holds an unlinked inode open, exhausts metadata with both
creation and writes, then checks close-time reclamation using the protected
cleanup reserve. Use a rootdir fixture with at least 64 MiB of imported data,
delete the imported files first, and run capacity with the released data space.
Check unmounted images with both independent checkers.

For recovery, run `hold new-directory` in the VM, wait for
`/tmp/btrfs-orphan-ready`, and reset the VM without closing the process's
descriptor. Check the unmounted image and confirm the orphan marker remains.
Writable mounting must clean it; use `recovered same-directory` afterwards.
Repeat with a nested subvolume, mounting a clean sibling first to test recovery
outside the selected hierarchy. Also reset during mount after observing a few
committed cleanup generations: intermediate inode byte accounting, extent and
free-space trees must check independently, and the next mount must finish.
Cover both metadata sizes and extent/bitmap free-space records. Root-tree and
nonregular linked-inode orphan rejection remain covered by orphan.py below.

rmdir.py uses create/verify/readonly for directory removal, open descriptors
and current directories, stale handles, rejection of new children in removed
directories, emptiness/type checks, flags, sticky and ordinary permissions,
hash collisions, concurrent removers, notifications, and recursive cleanup.
Its capacity phase checks protected removal at metadata exhaustion.
Run with both metadata sizes and independently check before remount verification.

rename.py uses create/verify/readonly for same-directory and cross-directory
moves, file/directory replacement, open destination descriptors, source handles,
hash collisions, ordinary/extended references, directory ancestry, cache
invalidation, flags, sticky and directory write permissions, and notifications.
Its races phase covers opposing parent locks, competing replacements with a
continuous lookup observer, and opposing directory moves. Reservation-pressure
retries check that each worker's source survives failure unchanged.
The capacity phase fills metadata with symlinks, verifies failed rename
atomicity, then closes a previously detached fragmented inode to release enough
space for ordinary unlink and eventual rename reuse. Use at least 256 MiB
with 16 KiB nodes and free-space trees; the conservative rename reservation
needs a sufficiently large metadata group even on an otherwise empty filesystem.
The packed phase on a fixture without EXTENDED_IREF checks same-item reference
reuse at capacity and atomic EMLINK across parents.
The subvol phase uses subvol.py's seed to check cross-tree moves and rejection
of subvolume-root rename/replacement; also run create inside a nested tree.
Run both node sizes with legacy and separate block groups and extent/bitmap
free space, checking independently between writable and read-only verification.
For replacement recovery, run hold, wait for /tmp/btrfs-rename-ready, and reset
the VM with the replaced inode still open. Check independently, mount writable
to recover its orphan, then run recovered and repeat the independent checks.

readdir.py uses seed/gaps on the host and exercise/verify in the VM.
Import its seed with `mkfs.btrfs --rootdir`, then run `gaps image` while
unmounted to renumber indexes and references above 32 bits with gaps.
Run both independent checks before mounting and after exercise. Verify after
writable and read-only remounts. Repeat with both metadata node sizes.
It covers small buffers, EOF, every saved cookie, seeks into gaps, multiple
leaves, file types and inode numbers, and creation after a reader reaches EOF.

statfs.py's `disk image` phase writes expected space counters as JSON using
the unmounted chunk and extent trees. Copy this output to the VM and use
`check mountpoint expected.json` before mutation and after remounts.
On a fixture with at most 32 MiB of free data space, run `exercise new-directory`
and `capacity same-directory`. These check live allocation, COW, truncate,
pending-sector cancellation, exact data exhaustion, ENOSPC, and reuse.
Unmount, run both independent checks, regenerate the expected JSON, then run
check and `verify same-directory` after writable and read-only remounts.
Repeat with both metadata node sizes and explicit holes.

filehandle.py uses `exercise new-directory saved.json`, with saved.json outside
the filesystem. It covers getfh/fhopen/fhstat, inode identity across mutations,
hard links and symlinks, directories, FIFOs, devices, advisory locks, flags,
privilege checks, and malformed/stale handles. Unmount and independently check,
then use verify/readonly on that directory after the respective remounts.
Run `stale saved.json` after the last view has closed, including after remount.
With subvol.py's seed mounted as disjoint left and right views, use
`subvol left-mount right-mount` to test nested trees, read-only subvolumes,
and forged handles that attempt to cross view boundaries.

orphan.py uses subvol.py's imported seed. While unmounted, use
`inject image treeid inode` with a nonroot directory inode selected from a
tree dump to add a pending directory-cleanup marker, or `inject image 1 259`
for a root-tree cleanup marker. Add an ordinary directory to the seed before
formatting for the directory-marker case. Run the independent
checks, then `reject device mountpoint` in the VM. Writable mounts, including
selection of a clean sibling, must return EOPNOTSUPP; read-only data inspection
must work. Repeat with both metadata sizes and verify writable mounting on a
clean fixture after the rejected attempts.

idle.py takes a new test directory and checks repeated fsync and mount sync
without pending changes, followed by further namespace and data mutation.
Run after writable remount of both a fresh image and one with a large extent
tree; sync must leave the mount writable.  Unmount and check independently.

symlink.py uses the same create/verify phases as namespace.py.  It covers
relative, absolute, dangling, chained and looping links, directory traversal,
short readlink buffers, maximum target/name lengths, hash collisions across
commits, permissions, concurrent creation, and directory accounting.  Run
both phases with an intervening unmount, independent check, and remount.

locks.py takes a new test directory and checks flock and POSIX record locks:
conflicts, shared locks, independent ranges, F_GETLK, partial unlock, SEEK_END,
blocking wakeup, duplicate descriptors, symlink aliases, and release on close
and process exit.  Run it under timeout, then unmount and check the image.
Its `readonly existing-test-directory` mode tests locks after a read-only
remount.  `reclaim mountpoint` creates two files and forcibly unmounts the
disposable filesystem with held locks and blocked waiters; run it from outside
the mount.  This mode checks that vnode reclaim interrupts the waiters.

kqueue.py uses `create new-test-directory` to test read/write readiness, EOF,
poll/select, vnode write/extend/attribute events, directory notifications,
subscription masks, descriptor close, and a reader woken by a child writer.
Use `readonly existing-test-directory` after a read-only remount.
`revoke mountpoint` forcibly unmounts with all three filters registered and
checks delivery and cleanup of revoke events.  Run this from outside the
mount under timeout, then check the unmounted image independently.

On a small disposable image, enospc.py fills the existing metadata block group
with empty files.  It checks that failed creation leaves no entry or parent
metadata changes, and that the filesystem remains writable and can sync.
For example:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/enospc.py /mnt/btrfs/full

Unmount and run the independent filesystem checker after this test as well.

hardlink.py uses create-extended/verify phases and a readonly phase after a
read-only remount.  It checks shared inode identity, data, attributes and
advisory locks, link counts, symlink aliases, concurrent links, hash collisions,
notifications, permissions, and extended inode-reference overflow, including
collisions across commits.  On images made with `mkfs.btrfs -O ^extref`, use
create instead of create-extended to check failure atomicity when a packed
inode-reference item fills.
The limit/verify-limit phases grow one inode to the advertised `LINK_MAX`,
check every alias after remount, and ensure the next link fails atomically.
Run limit in two separate directories on the same image to exercise metadata
allocation past the 64 MiB superblock mirror, then unmount and run both host
checks.  Writable remount and idle sync/unmount also exercise commits without
pending namespace mutations.

lookup.py checks hash lookup of independently imported directory items.  On
the host, create a fixture and import it into an unmounted disposable image:

    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/lookup.py seed /tmp/btrfs-lookup
    mkfs.btrfs -f -n 4096 -s 4096 -m dup -d single \
        -O ^free-space-tree,^block-group-tree \
        --rootdir /tmp/btrfs-lookup --subvol rw:subvol /path/to/image

In the VM, mount the image and run:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/lookup.py verify /mnt/btrfs

Repeat after read-only remount.  This covers packed hash collisions, long and
UTF-8 names, hard links, symlinks, missing names, subvolume entries and parent
traversal.

subvol.py tests simultaneous writable mounts of disjoint subvolume hierarchies.
Seed an unmounted image on the host:

    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/subvol.py seed /tmp/btrfs-subvol
    mkfs.btrfs -f -b 1G -n 4096 -s 4096 -m dup -d single \
        -O ^free-space-tree,^block-group-tree --rootdir /tmp/btrfs-subvol \
        --subvol rw:left --subvol rw:left/nested \
        --subvol ro:left/frozen --subvol rw:right /path/to/image

Read IDs from `btrfs inspect-internal dump-tree -t root /path/to/image`.
In the VM, with the image initially unmounted, supply the IDs in that order:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/subvol.py exercise \
        /dev/sd1c /mnt/subvol-test 256 257 258 259

This mounts and unmounts its own views. It covers concurrent writes in three
trees, stat identity, parent/getcwd traversal, cross-tree hard-link rejection,
read-only tree and mount policy, overlap rejection in both directions, invalid
IDs, busy and forced unmount, surviving views, last-view teardown, and racing
overlapping attaches. Run both independent checks with all views unmounted,
then mount the top-level tree read-only and run `subvol.py verify mountpoint`.
Repeat with 16 KiB metadata. Rebuild mount_btrfs with the matching sys/mount.h
when changing kernels: its mount arguments now include the selected tree ID.

send_receive.py provides targeted OpenBSD/Linux interoperability fixtures.
Use separate scratch filesystems for source and destination, and unmount a
device in one VM before mounting it in the other. Always use timeout for
OpenBSD driver commands. On OpenBSD, create a writable subvolume, run `seed`
on its mounted directory, snapshot it read-only, run `mutate` on the writable
source, and take a second read-only snapshot. Send the first with
`btrfs send -f full.stream mountpoint full`, and the second with
`btrfs send -p full -f incremental.stream mountpoint incremental`.
Receive both into another filesystem with `btrfs receive -f file mountpoint /`.
Use `compare source received` to check data, hardlink topology, node types,
device numbers, modes, ownership, mtimes, and xattrs. Symlink permission bits
are excluded because Linux streams do not carry them.

Repeat with Linux's btrfs send/receive in both directions, including an
incremental directory swap, truncation, hardlink changes, sparse growth, and
binary/directory xattrs. Run unmounted `btrfs check --readonly --check-data-csum`
and repeat comparisons after remounting. Run `readonly directory` in both VMs
after mounting native-created snapshots and received roots: Linux must retain
the root-item read-only flag. Use 4 KiB and 16 KiB metadata fixtures.
On Linux, run `clones writable-source` after the incremental fixture and send
both a full snapshot and a snapshot relative to the prior parent. These must
exercise CLONE commands referring to the receive tree and to its parent.
With only an unrelated child mounted, repeat native send/receive outside that
view and verify that temporary mounts disappear. `lifecycle mountpoint path`
and `send_lifecycle mountpoint source` check signal interruption in that
arrangement. With tree 5 mounted,
`busy mountpoint path` checks finalization with an active writer, and
`malformed mountpoint path` checks CRC/TLV validation, truncation, unsupported
commands/versions, and lexical/symlink escapes. Those phases leave incomplete
writable test subvolumes intentionally.

subvolume.py tests native administration through /dev/btrfs-control and the
installed btrfs command. The runner cases `subvolume`, `subvolume-orphans`,
`subvolume-zstd`, and `subvolume-capacity` cover its automated phases; select
them with `--select 'subvolume*'`. The capacity case fixes the image at 128 MiB
with 16 KiB nodes and single data regardless of the selected layout.
On an empty writable top-level mount run `create`,
then unmount and run both independent checks. Remount and run `verify`.
Read the ID of `latest` from `btrfs subvolume list mountpoint`, unmount, and
mount only that ID with `mount_btrfs -o rw -s ID`. Run `child` there to test
filesystem-relative paths outside the mounted view, sibling snapshots and
deletion, and refusal to delete the selected view. Unmount/check again, then
mount tree 5 and run `final`. The phases verify shared metadata/data COW,
read-only snapshots, hard links, source deletion, final reference reclamation,
namespace errors, active-file and nested-subvolume deletion rejection, and
path reconstruction after ancestor-directory rename.

Run `boundaries` and `race` on a top-level writable mount for nested snapshot
boundary directories and administration concurrent with file writes, fsync,
creation, and unlink. Unmount and check afterward. Repeat at 4 KiB and 16 KiB
metadata sizes. `abi` checks malformed requests, descriptor access, and
privilege checks on an inherited control descriptor. `orphans` snapshots an
open-unlinked file into a read-only tree; unmount/check, remount writable, and
run `orphan_verify` to check internal recovery and deletion.
On a fresh 128 MiB image with 16 KiB nodes, `capacity` checks that complete
deletion reservation fails with ENOSPC while preserving the namespace and
data, then verifies continued writes and deletion after individual unlinks.
Unmount and run both independent checks afterward.
For compressed extents, run `seed directory` on the host,
format an unmounted image with `--rootdir directory --subvol rw:imported
--compress zstd`, then run `compressed` in the VM. After independent checks,
run `compressed_verify` on writable and read-only remounts. Always put VM
driver commands under a timeout.

For snapshot publication recovery, create and sync a source subvolume with
known data, enter DDB through the monitor NMI, set a breakpoint at
btrfs_write_super_mirrors, and continue. Start a snapshot command and reset
the VM when the breakpoint stops its commit before superblock publication.
Check the unmounted image independently, then remount writable and verify
the source data, absence of the unpublished snapshot, and continued writes,
snapshot creation, and deletion. Unmount and check again.

ipc.py uses create/verify phases and a readonly phase after a read-only
remount. It covers FIFO and Unix-domain socket creation, hard-link aliases,
blocking/nonblocking pipe I/O, EOF, kqueue readiness, socket connections,
permissions, hash collisions, and directory accounting. Run the independent
checks between mounts as above. IPC and device tests also check that explicit
truncate/ftruncate leaves special-file sizes and timestamps unchanged on both
writable and read-only mounts.

devices.py uses create/verify, readonly, and nodev phases for character/block
device creation, device-number persistence, hard links, attributes, permissions,
null/zero I/O, kqueue, advisory locks, BPF clones, mounted-device aliases, and
unknown device numbers. It uses OpenBSD/amd64 device assignments and never
writes disk devices. Its `reclaim mountpoint` phase forcibly unmounts with
open null/BPF descriptors and checks device-vnode survival and cleanup.
Its `race new-directory` phase races alias creation and fsync against attribute
changes and fsync on an existing device vnode to check transaction lock order.
Import its seed fixture (created as root on the host)
with --rootdir, then run `fix-seed image` on the unmounted image: mkfs.btrfs 7.0
sets imported rdev fields to zero, so this phase uses btrfs-corrupt-block to
install known device numbers. Use seed-verify to check device-number decoding
and rejection of majors outside OpenBSD's range. Run with 4 KiB and 16 KiB
metadata, with independent checks between mounts.

inline.py uses seed on the host to prepare a rootdir fixture, write in the VM,
then verify after unmount/check/remount. It covers conversion of imported
uncompressed inline files, partial overwrites, append, sector boundaries,
sparse extension, cached reads, concurrent writers, hard links, and repeated
COW after fsync. Its capacity phase fills existing data space on a small
image and checks that a failed sparse inline conversion preserves data and
attributes and leaves the mount writable. Unmount and check afterward.
Import a fresh seed image with --compress zlib and use reject to check
compressed-inline write rejection without attribute changes. Zlib reads are
not implemented, so unmount, use btrfs restore on the host, and run verify-seed
on the restored directory to check the unchanged data independently.

Use inline.py's seed-zstd/write-zstd/verify-zstd phases with `--compress zstd`
to cover conversion of compressed inline prefixes, overwrites, sparse writes,
and truncate growth across sector boundaries. Its capacity-zstd phase
checks ENOSPC atomicity for both sparse writes and growth. Repeat with and
without `NO_HOLES`, and check/restore independently between mounts. Use
verify-restored-zstd for restored data, since restore does not recreate links.

grow.py uses seed on the host, exercise in the VM, then verify and readonly
after unmount/check/remount. Import the seed using the rootdir command above.
It covers sparse truncate/ftruncate growth of imported inline and regular
files, created files, sector boundaries, ordered writes, hard-link aliases,
concurrent operations, cached and mapped reads, huge holes, permissions, file
size limits, and notifications. The capacity phase on a fresh small seed
image fills data space and checks failed inline/tail COW without inode changes,
followed by successful growth of an empty file. Use seed_compressed for
compression fixtures and reject/verify_compressed_seed for rejection tests;
repeat uncompressed exercise/verify with `-O ^no-holes`. Independently check
and restore the image between phases. A zlib seed also supports
compressed/verify_compressed (verify on the restored copy), which grows past
an aligned compressed extent and writes the new sparse tail.

With the compressed fixture imported using `--compress zstd`, zstd/verify_zstd
also exercise compressed reads and sector replacements on writable mounts
with unrelated pending changes, hard links, attributes, and repeated
COW of the uncompressed tail. Run verify_zstd after both writable and read-only
remounts, with independent filesystem, checksum, and mirror checks between.

shrink.py uses seed/exercise/verify/readonly with independent checks between
mounts. It covers imported and created files, zero/partial/aligned truncation,
live read mappings, regrowth without stale data, pending-sector cancellation,
split data references, hard links, O_TRUNC, flags, notifications, and huge holes.
Repeat with 4 KiB and 16 KiB nodes, explicit holes (`-O ^no-holes`), and imported
NODATASUM/NODATACOW files. Its capacity phase on a fresh seed with a small data
block group checks data and metadata reservation failures without file changes,
followed by successful sparse shrinking and fragmented/whole-extent deletion.

truncate_batches.py uses create/verify for linked and open-unlinked fragmented
files, hard links, partial EOF, mapped reads, byte accounting, and zero regrowth.
Its capacity phase on a small device exhausts metadata and the smaller write
reservation margin before truncating a linked file and reusing its space.
Use capacity_verify after remount.
For recovery, run prepare, set a DDB breakpoint on btrfs_cleanup_inode, then run
truncate and reset when it stops after target-size publication. Check the
unmounted image and marker, mount writable, and run recovered. Repeat after
several batch commits to exercise a persisted deletion cursor. Test both node
sizes and explicit holes, with independent checks and both remount modes.

shrink_compressed.py uses seed/exercise/verify_metadata with `--compress zlib`
fixtures and exercise_zstd with `--compress zstd`. It tests O_TRUNC without decoding,
deletion at compressed extent boundaries, preserved prefix references,
sparse regrowth, and failure atomicity when retaining zlib overlap.
Run verify in the VM for Zstd, and on host `btrfs restore` output for all codecs.
Use the independent filesystem/checksum and mirror checks between mounts.

zstd_cow.py uses seed/exercise/verify with `--compress zstd` imports. It covers
sector splits at nonzero decoded offsets, concurrent overwrites, partial shrink
and growth, live read mappings, hard links, repeated reference splitting and
final drops, and ordered replacement. Run restore on host `btrfs restore`
output. Its capacity phase on a fresh small-data-group fixture checks atomic
ENOSPC for overwrites and partial EOF COW, then successful aligned shrinking.
Repeat with both metadata node sizes and explicit holes, checking all metadata
copies, data checksums, and writable/read-only remounts.

holes.py uses seed/write/verify to test splitting stored holes at their start,
middle, and end, repeated splits, writes across mapping boundaries, and sparse
extension. Import with `-O ^no-holes` and run both independent checks between
write and verify; repeat with `NO_HOLES`. Its capacity phase on a fresh small
image without `NO_HOLES` fills metadata and checks that failed growth and sparse
writes preserve data and inode state and allow sync without becoming read-only.

nodatasum.py tests imported NODATASUM/NODATACOW files alongside checksummed
controls. Run `seed directory`, then `format directory image 4096 no-holes`
on the host while unmounted; use `16384 holes` for explicit-hole coverage.
In the VM run `exercise mountpoint`, then unmount and run the independent
checks and `disk image` to check inode flags and checksum coverage. Run
`verify mountpoint` after writable/read-only remounts and `verify-restored`
on a host-restored copy. Its `capacity mountpoint` phase checks write/growth
ENOSPC atomicity for every policy. Coverage includes inline conversion, split
extents, ordered and committed COW, sparse growth, hard links, and chflags.

inherit.py seeds normal, NODATACOW, and xattr parent directories. Import its
`seed directory` fixture with `--inode-flags nodatacow:nocow` and
`--inode-flags nodatacow:xattr`. Run `exercise mountpoint` in the VM, unmount
and check independently (including `disk image`), then use `more mountpoint`
after writable remount and `verify mountpoint` after read-only remount.
It checks nested/concurrent creation, regular and special inodes, checksum
policy, hard links between policies, noninherited user flags, and continued
rejection of creation under parents with xattrs.
